  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Regeneration of shea parklands and transformation of shea value chains for improved livelihoods of vulnerable rural communities in Burkina Faso.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Shea trees (Vitellaria paradoxa)  are the most common parkland tree species in Burkina Faso representing 80% of socio-economic value species, as it constitutes one of its main national commodities. While the species is known for its resilience to long dry seasons, shea trees are only able to survive extreme weather events once it reaches fruit production maturity, which takes up to 20 years.  Natural stands are also being lost to cutting of mature trees for the energy needs, vulnerability to pests, and disease the slow growth of the regenerated species and the lack of maintenance of the young plants. The loss of Shea parklands and the decrease in fruit availability and the kernels# quality due to climate change effects can have a devastating loss of income of communities who rely on Shea during the agricultural #lean period#. The proposed project seeks to address the impact of climate change on Burkina Faso#s aging shea parklands using climate smart agroforestry to reduce emissions, and plant climate adapted Shea trees to support regeneration of shea parks to ensure sustainable ecosystem services, improve livelihood generation of surrounding rural communities, and greening the production process of shea butter reducing biomass energy consumption.  The project aims to achieve sustainable land management practice</narrative>
  </description>
